# SET-PIECE: the bunker. THE OTHER KIND OF HIGH GROUND.
#
# One unit up, three cells across, and TWO ramps onto it -- everything the
# watchpost is not. Where that one asks you to commit three seconds of helpless
# climbing for a tall perch, this one is high ground you can take in a stride and
# fight from immediately, and so can anything else.
#
# THE ASCENDER IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE, and it is worth having both in the
# library rather than one tuned in between. A ramp is walkable the moment you
# reach it and you can shoot the entire way up, so a bunker is contested ground:
# the party can hold it, and a rusher can walk straight onto it. A ladder is a
# commitment, so the watchpost is a decision. Two pieces, two answers to "what
# does high ground cost".
#
# NOTHING IS AUTHORED ON IT. A bunker with a turret on it would be a watchpost
# with a gentler climb -- what makes this one interesting is that it is EMPTY and
# both sides want it. The lane either side stays at deck level, so walking past
# is always an option and taking the high ground is always a choice.
#
# TWO RAMPS, NOT ONE, and they are the reason it reads as a place rather than as
# a bump: a single ramp makes the bunker a dead end you back out of, while two
# make it a route with a decision at each end.

name = piece_bunker
base_height = 0
width = 5
length = 6
piece_exit = 0
tags = foot, piece, environmental

